is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dswa1, dswa2
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the VRRP/keepalived gateway for the WLAN (VLAN 40, 10.6.0.0/24). The VRRP control-plane is down on both distribution switches.

Mechanism: With keepalived stopped, no VRRP advertisements or failover occur. The current master retains the virtual IP (VIP) address, so some traffic still works, but clients can intermittently lose default-gateway reachability (e.g., after ARP changes or a brief flap) because there is no active VRRP to refresh gratuitous ARP or fail to the peer.

Localization: On dswa2, interface br0.40 has 10.6.0.3/24 and the VRRP VIP 10.6.0.1/32; dswa1 br0.40 has 10.6.0.2/24 only. The laptop (10.6.0.100) is defaulting via 10.6.0.1 and can presently ping it, showing dswa2 is acting as master. However, on both dswa1 and dswa2 the keepalived service is not running: “keepalived.service … Active: inactive (dead)” (confirmed via systemctl status on both switches).

Root cause: keepalived daemons are stopped on both dswa1 and dswa2, leaving the WLAN VRRP VIP in a stale, unmanaged state.

Corrective fix: Restart keepalived on both distribution switches to restore VRRP. Recommended order: start on dswa1, then dswa2, or simply restart on both; the configured priorities will elect the master and resume advertisements. Commands: systemctl restart keepalived (and ensure it is enabled). After restart, verify 10.6.0.1 is owned by the intended master and that VRRP adverts/ARP are active; re-check client reachability.
=== END ===